202: ## Step numbering: method versus skill
203: 
204: | Diagram | `docs/WORKFLOW_DESIGN_METHOD.md` | `.claude/skills/workflow-design/SKILL.md` |
205: |---|---|---|
206: | 1, top | 3.0 understand the challenge (six whys, provisional category), §2's tiny test reads it | "First: understand the challenge", then "Then: is it tiny?" |
207: | 1 | 3.1 readiness, 3.2 algorithm and piece labels, 3.3 spots, 3.4 size, 3.5 loops, 3.6 graph, 3.7 sequential first | steps 1–7, same order |
208: | 1, at DESIGN | 3.4 "Default-first design" and the 20% ledger | step 4's default-first sentences; `references/task-types.md` |
209: | 1, bottom | 3.6, the node that builds, the baseline, the backlog | step 8, "the run ends at the acceptance criteria" |
210: | 2 | 3.1, "Blueprint check — part of readiness" | inside step 1 |
211: | 3 | 3.5 | step 5 |
212: | 4 | 3.6, the five-things table | "Every node carries five things" |
213: | — | **3.8** "Running a node: executors are tools, timers, pass-back, no polling" (added 2026-10-02) | **no step.** Node running is execution detail, not a design decision, so the skill keeps it as reference: step 1 sends the reader to `references/executor-kinds.md` for the canary, read-only vs write, and the fallback rule. This row exists because the table above once omitted 3.8 silently, which reads as if the method had 8 sections and the skill 8 steps, one each |
